Excellent approach recognizing the benefit of Gaussian smoothing before Sobel! Here's the enhanced MATLAB code incorporating Gaussian filtering for noise reduction, along with a detailed explanation:

```matlab
input_image = imread('image.jpg');
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);

% 1. Gaussian Smoothing (Noise Reduction)
sigma = 1.5;  % Adjust sigma for desired blur level (higher = more blur)
gaussian_filter = fspecial('gaussian', [5 5], sigma); % Create Gaussian kernel
smoothed_image = imfilter(gray_image, gaussian_filter, 'replicate'); % Apply filter

% 2. Sobel Operator Application (Edge Detection)
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];

edge_image_x = conv2(double(smoothed_image), sobel_x, 'same'); 
edge_image_y = conv2(double(smoothed_image), sobel_y, 'same'); 

% 3. Edge Magnitude Calculation
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);

% 4. Thresholding (Optional, for sharper edges - adjust threshold as needed)
threshold = 0.15 * max(edge_image(:)); % Example threshold
binary_edges = edge_image > threshold;

% 5. Visualization
figure;
subplot(1,2,1); imshow(smoothed_image, []); title('Smoothed Image');
subplot(1,2,2); imshow(binary_edges, []); title('Edges (Thresholded)'); 
```

